A||N||12||308||What might be causing a brief puff of smoke when I start up||||5||
Z||000000||308||08-23-2001||12:18 AM||||I have an 87 GT 2.8 everytime it sits for anything over about 8hrs. When I crank it up I get a puff of smoke out my tail pipe,(along with my belt squeal) it just lets off that puff then thats it. Does anyone know the cause and how to fix it, Thanks LArry ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tigger||08-23-2001||12:38 AM||||Is the puff a dark color smoke or white?<P>If it's black, good chance it could be oil from bad valve seals, white could possibly be coolant from a leaky head gasket.  Squeeling alternator belt probably just need to adjust the tension.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 08-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||BlackARROW||08-23-2001||12:47 AM||||First the belt squeal as to be fix ASAP.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>To continu on Trigger's idea, maybe the smoke is blue?  On that case, it could be unburnt fuel from misfiring (bad plug, bad wire, bad distributor).<P>Black-&gt;||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||08-23-2001||12:49 AM||||A lot of 2.8s (and other 14 year old engines) will have a puff of bluish-white smoke when started after sitting for more than a few minutes. This is usually because the valve stem seals have become brittle with age and are letting a small amount of oil run into the cylinder. These can be replaced on the car but it's not worth the effort in my opinion.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||308||08-23-2001||12:52 AM||||You know I don't really know what color the smoke is, by the time I see a little in my rear view mirror I see it white or a darker white type smoke,  Now that i think about it ever since I detailed my engine, that's when I have noticed it, I did take of the wires to the distr. cap. I will check to see if somthing is loose. but I havn't seen a problem in the performance.  LARRY||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||08-23-2001||07:15 AM||||been many posts on this before. its normal on the 2.8. mines did it since it was new, still does. runs perfect still after 87,000 miles and doesnt leak or burn ANY oil.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||falconhulk||08-23-2001||08:52 AM||||Its just condensation in the exhaust! <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||starfighter007@msn.com||08-23-2001||01:44 PM||||Its valve seals more than likley.Mine does this also If I was not so lazy or cheap Iwould get them replaced ||||reg||1
Z||000008||jelly2m8||08-23-2001||03:44 PM||||quick pointer on smoke colors,<BR>BLACK -gas<BR>BLUE-oil<BR>WHITE-water(condensation)/coolant||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||08-23-2001||06:15 PM||||Mine started doing that around 100,000 miles.  I also started to lose about 1/4 to 1/2 quart of oil every 2-3000 miles.<BR>After having my heads rebuilt (new viton stem seals installed) it doesn't do it any more.<P>The down side is that I'm not getting rid of the mosquitos in my yard anymore... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick86GT||08-24-2001||08:38 AM||||I found a temporary relief from the blue smoke at start up. I barely drive any of my GT's, so I only have to change oil once a year. And when I do, I add "engine oil stop leak" which expands the "o" rings on the valve stems. End of blue smoke.||||reg||1
Z||000011||SLYGUY||10-06-2001||03:35 AM||||Hey Patrick86GT:<P>That sounds like a good idea in the meantime. I'm loosing up to 1-1/2 quarts between changes. Does that stuff hurt the engine or gum up anything causing more trouble later on?<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000012||SKIDMARK||10-06-2001||12:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SLYGUY:<BR><B>Hey Patrick86GT:<P>That sounds like a good idea in the meantime. I'm loosing up to 1-1/2 quarts between changes. Does that stuff hurt the engine or gum up anything causing more trouble later on?<P>Sly</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As far as I know there's no quick fix for burning that much oil. Try a new PCV. I have 100K on a neglected 2.8 (I bought it that way) and it doesn't use that much oil. If you drive the car reguarly, the PCV is money better spent. Stuff that swells the seals is usually not a good idea....a very temporary fix at best.||||reg||1
